    S('Assessment','Integrated IB-style precision audit','student','Number sets, significant figures, bounds, error and interpretation in one coherent task',`
      <div class="nf-ib-task"><div class="nf-ib-head"><span>IB-STYLE INTEGRATED TASK · 12 MARKS</span><b>A circular optical component has measured diameter \\(d=4.20\\) cm to 3 significant figures. A model uses \\(A=\\pi d^2/4\\).</b></div><ol class="nf-ib-parts"><li><b>State</b> the smallest number set containing \\(\\pi\\), and explain why \\(3.142\\) belongs to a different smallest set. [2]</li><li><b>Write down</b> the lower and upper endpoints for \\(d\\). [2]</li><li><b>Calculate</b> lower and upper endpoints for the area. [3]</li><li>The nominal model gives \\(13.85\\text{ cm}^2\\). <b>Determine</b> the maximum possible percentage difference from a true area consistent with the measurement. [3]</li><li><b>Comment</b> on whether reporting the area as \\(13.854423\\text{ cm}^2\\) is appropriate. [2]</li></ol><textarea class="student-note tall" data-note="nf-integrated-ib-task" aria-label="Integrated precision audit working"></textarea><details class="solution-reveal"><summary>Markscheme outline</summary><p>(a) \\(\\pi\\in\\mathbb R\\setminus\\mathbb Q\\); \\(3.142\\in\\mathbb Q\\). (b) \\(4.195\\le d<4.205\\). (c) use \\(\\pi(4.195)^2/4\\) and \\(\\pi(4.205)^2/4\\). (d) compare the nominal area with both extremes and divide the larger absolute difference by the corresponding accepted/true reference specified in the method. (e) six decimal places are not justified by a 3-s.f. diameter.</p></details></div>`)
